ML-Based Utility Evaluation: Random Forest Classification (Network_Type)
This is a privacy-utility tradeoff analysis for a Random Forest classifier predicting Network_Type, evaluated across several anonymization/privacy-preserving techniques. Let me break down each of the three panels:

1. Privacy vs. ML Accuracy Tradeoff (Left Panel)
This scatter plot shows how different anonymization techniques balance privacy (x-axis: % records satisfying k≥5) against ML accuracy (y-axis):

Technique	Privacy (k≥5 %)	Accuracy (%)	Assessment
Raw Baseline	~0%	~45.7%	No privacy, moderate accuracy
Data Swapping	~40%	~42%	Low privacy, worst accuracy
Cell Suppression	~60%	~45.5%	Moderate privacy, near-baseline accuracy
Generalization	~70%	~43.5%	Good privacy, slight accuracy drop
Row Suppression k-Anon (k=5)	~75%	~43.5%	Good privacy, similar to generalization
Attribute Suppression	~97%	~55.3%	Best of both — highest privacy AND accuracy
Generalization + Suppression	~100%	~55.3%	Best of both — near-perfect privacy AND highest accuracy
Key Insight: Attribute Suppression and Generalization + Suppression are clear winners — they sit in the top-right corner, meaning they achieve both the highest accuracy AND the best privacy. This is unusual and noteworthy.

2. ML Accuracy with Privacy Overlay (Center Panel)
This bar+line chart overlays accuracy (bars) with privacy (purple line/diamonds):

Green bars = ML Accuracy (%)
Purple line = k≥5 Privacy (%)
Red/pink annotations = Change from baseline (45.7%)
Standout results:

Attribute Suppression and Generalization + Suppression both achieve 55.3% accuracy (+21.2% above baseline) with 97–100% privacy
Data Swapping performs worst: 41.2% accuracy (−9.9%) with only ~60% privacy
Row Suppression k-Anon (k=5) achieves 100% privacy but accuracy drops to 43.4% (−5.0%)
3. Feature Importance — Raw Data Model (Right Panel)
This reveals why removing certain features can actually improve accuracy:

Feature	Importance
Timestamp	0.295 (highest)
RAI	0.291
LAI	0.287
Device_Type	0.049
PLMN	0.038
MMNC	0.022
MCC	0.018
TMSI	0.000
TLLI	0.000
LMSI	0.000
Key Insight: The top 3 features (Timestamp, RAI, LAI) dominate with ~0.29 each. Features like TMSI, TLLI, and LMSI have zero importance. When Attribute Suppression removes these noisy/irrelevant features, the model actually performs better because it eliminates noise that was confusing the classifier.

Overall Conclusions
Attribute Suppression and Generalization + Suppression are the optimal strategies — they paradoxically increase accuracy by ~10% over baseline while achieving near-perfect privacy.
The accuracy improvement happens because suppressing low-importance features (TMSI, TLLI, LMSI, MCC, etc.) reduces noise in the model.
Data Swapping is the worst technique here — it degrades accuracy without providing strong privacy.
This is a case where privacy and utility are not in conflict — the right anonymization technique acts as implicit feature selection, improving model performance.